00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:52.580
Now, before we get into building permits
themselves, a bit of background
understanding is required.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:04:00.700
We need to understand what this housing
report is all about, the Housing Starts
Report that comes out every month.

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:06.180
So the building permits number comes from
the U.S. Housing Starts Report.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:11.480
The report also includes a number for
housing completions.

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:19.300
And each of these three variables, these
three data sets, have a different
definition.

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:25.400
We should understand that definition
first, so you can understand the report
better.

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:33.960
It essentially breaks down the entire
process of a residential housing unit
being developed or built.

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:39.840
So we have three stages for a residential
home to be built.

00:04:40.260 --> 00:04:42.400
We have a building permit being
authorized.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.380
We have the project being started, a
housing start.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:50.900
And when the house is complete, we have a
housing completion.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:05:01.440
And the way we define these data sets
individually is basically from a time
perspective, okay?

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:09.300
A building permit is a leading indicator
because it leads the housing start.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:18.200
A housing start is a coincidence indicator
because it tells you what is going on at
that moment in time in the economy.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:28.320
A housing completion is a lagging
indicator because once the home is
complete and it's built, it's finished.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.320
The project is over.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:39.080
So throughout the time series, the housing
start is when there's economic activity
occurring.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:48.960
The building permit precedes the housing
start, so the building permit is when the
project is authorized.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.180
So what is the definition of a building
permit?

00:05:53.180 --> 00:06:02.140
Well, the building has basically received
planning permission by the local state
planning department.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:12.500
For a housing start, the foundations of
the building have been laid, the project
has started, the economic activity has
started.

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:19.340
Housing completion, the structure and the
major interior work has been completed.

00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:28.140
So there's probably still minor work to
do, but it's not essential, and the home
is currently being marketed for sale.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:36.940
So another way to look at this, a building
permit is when the paperwork for the
project is put together.

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:43.740
A housing start is when the physical
commodities become the house.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:45.740
What do we mean by that?

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:47.540
Well, the foundations go in.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:48.540
Well, the foundations go in.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:58.540
There's bricks, cement, steel, iron ore,
copper wiring, electrics, components, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:07:07.340
This is when physical commodities, the
economic activity starts and the house is
built.

00:07:07.340 --> 00:07:11.540
Physical commodities turn into the
property.

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:20.340
Then the housing completion, this is when
the project is complete and the economic
activity is stopping.

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:25.140
So it's paperwork, physical commodities
become houses.

00:07:26.420 --> 00:07:29.460
The project stops, housing completion.

00:07:30.260 --> 00:07:34.900
So we have the paperwork, which is the
leading indicator.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:41.380
We have the physical commodity
transformation into the home, which is the
housing start.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.540
That's economic activity that's occurring
in the economy right now.

00:07:46.260 --> 00:07:48.980
And we have the housing completion.

00:07:48.980 --> 00:07:51.220
And that is a lagging indicator.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:53.460
So we go from paperwork, leading.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:56.500
Housing start, coincidence.

00:07:57.140 --> 00:07:58.980
Project completed, lagging.

00:08:00.660 --> 00:08:07.060
Now, given that this is actually a time
series, and as traders and portfolio
managers, we want to know

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:16.820
what the length of time is between a
building permit authorization, a housing
start, and a housing completion.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:26.420
Because we want to know what length of
time of building permits leading indicator
is leading the economy by,

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:32.340
and leading the coincidence and the
lagging indicator.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:38.580
Well, we can get this data if we go to the
Census Bureau website.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:48.500
And if we look at the URL extension there,
so construction forward slash nrc forward
slash length of time.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:59.620
And you can see we can look at the length
of time and get the data set, the length
of time that it takes for an authorization
to start.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:07.620
And we can also get the length of time
that it takes, get the data for a start
becoming a completion.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:21.780
So let's have a look at the data for a
building permit being authorized and the
length of time it takes for a housing
start to occur.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:33.700
If you look at the screen here, you can
get this PDF download from the Census
Bureau website in the URL that I just
mentioned.

00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:52.420
And we're looking here at the percentage
distribution of new privately owned
residential buildings started in permit
issuing places in the year of 2020 by the
number of months from authorization.

00:09:52.420 --> 00:10:04.100
So the number of months it takes for a
housing start to start from the moment of
authorization across the United States in
the year 2020.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.580
So let's have a look at the percentage
distribution here.

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:22.180
Now, there are states that allow
developers to break ground and start a
project before a building permit
authorization is issued.

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:30.100
The first part you're looking at here, if
we look at the headline numbers of the
United States, is exactly this.

00:10:30.100 --> 00:10:42.900
So prior to or at the same time or in the
same month as the authorization or the
building permit is issued, this comes out
at 43%.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:11:02.100
So you can see in a lot of states around
the US, developers from the local state
planning department are allowed to break
ground and they're allowed to start their
project either slightly before or in the
month of the building permit being
authorized.

00:11:02.100 --> 00:11:06.100
This comes in at 43%.

00:11:06.100 --> 00:11:16.100
If we look at the rest of the percentage
distribution for the 2020 year, we can see
one month comes in at 37%.

00:11:16.100 --> 00:11:22.100
So that's one month after the building
permit has been authorized.

00:11:22.100 --> 00:11:25.820
So one month comes in at 37%.

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:29.100
Then two months comes in at 10%.

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:32.020
Three months comes in at 4%.

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:37.400
So I've added those up and we get to 51%.

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:45.520
Basically, in the one to three month time
period after a building permit is
authorized.

00:11:46.180 --> 00:11:49.100
And yes, guys, it's not a coincidence.

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:56.820
Housing developers are trading one to
three month portfolios as well.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:08.180
Additionally, you can see that projects
starting from authorization, four months
or more comes in at 6%.

00:12:08.180 --> 00:12:20.780
So if we add the one, two, three and four
months or more together, 57% is one month
plus, but the meet obviously is in the one
to three month period.

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:41.500
So essentially, if we add the 51% and the
43%, 94% of projects are started either
around the time when the permit is issued
by the State Planning Department or within
three months.

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:47.220
We can also look at completions.

00:12:47.220 --> 00:12:59.220
And if you download the PDF there for
completions, whatever time period you're
sitting in, but we've got completions here
for 2020.

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:05.940
We can look at the percentage distribution
for the time periods for completions
coming from starts.

00:13:05.940 --> 00:13:14.940
So started projects that are actually
getting completed and how long it takes.

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:28.760
So if we look at the headline data for
2020 across the whole of the US, we can
see we've got 16% is three months or less.

00:13:28.760 --> 00:13:39.040
So it takes three months for a housing
start to become a completion that's 16% of
projects.

00:13:39.040 --> 00:13:46.480
Next, we've got four to six months, seven
to nine months, and 10 to 12 months.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:53.120
And if we add that up together, that shows
a time lag of four to 12 months.

00:13:53.120 --> 00:14:01.320
And then in the minority of cases, we have
projects going from start to completion in
13 months or more.

00:14:01.320 --> 00:14:03.840
And that comes in at 9%.

00:14:03.840 --> 00:14:11.640
So the meet is obviously in that period in
four to 12 months, but really it's four to
nine months.

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:23.000
So we can actually get conclusions here on
the actual time lag from permits to starts
and from starts to completions.

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:38.040
And therefore, how much time permits are
leading starts and how much time starts
are leading completions, but also how much
time permits are leading completions.

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:41.320
So permits leading starts.

00:14:41.320 --> 00:14:44.800
We're looking at three to four months.

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:46.760
Where does the four months come from?

00:14:46.760 --> 00:15:03.000
Well, that's because in the data, the
state planning departments in some states
allow developers to actually break ground
at the same time or one month before
permits are issued.

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:08.600
So to be prudent, we're using three to
four months leading housing starts.

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:12.840
So permits leading housing starts by three
to four months.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:15.320
What about starts leading completions?

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:16.840
Well, we've just looked at that.

00:15:16.840 --> 00:15:18.040
It's on the screen.

00:15:18.040 --> 00:15:23.320
We're looking at the vast majority of
times in the four to 12 month window.

00:15:23.880 --> 00:15:31.880
So what does that mean for us as traders
and portfolio managers when we're looking
at building permits as a leading
indicator?

00:15:32.280 --> 00:15:43.320
Well, it means permits are leading housing
starts by three to four months and permits
are leading completions by anything
between eight to 16 months.

00:15:43.720 --> 00:15:48.280
So it's obviously an extremely useful
leading indicator.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:54.760
Now, what does all this actually mean for
us as traders and portfolio managers?

00:15:55.400 --> 00:16:07.400
Well, this is part of the reason why so
many players in the market misunderstand
or misinterpret what the building permits
number actually means,

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:19.160
because it infers a lot of useful
information for us, but people, a lot of
people in the market don't really
understand what it infers.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:37.480
We are looking at how many building
permits have been issued by state planning
departments in the U.S. at any one moment
in time in a particular month for the
previous month.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:45.640
And those building permits can go up month
on month and they can go down month on
month.

00:16:45.640 --> 00:16:49.160
And we look at the change in that as well.

00:16:49.160 --> 00:16:53.880
So we look at the total number and we look
at the changes month on month.

00:16:53.880 --> 00:17:01.560
So by definition, the building permits
number as a leading indicator tells us
several things.

00:17:01.560 --> 00:17:10.760
The first thing it tells us is it gives us
a very good gauge on developer sentiment,
on housing developer sentiment.

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:21.560
So it tells us whether or not developers
are bullish or bearish on the prospects of
future home sales.

00:17:22.760 --> 00:17:27.720
Developers have to physically make the
application for building permits.

00:17:28.280 --> 00:17:38.600
So more applications for building permits
means, from a developer sentiment
perspective, an expectation of future
sales.

00:17:39.240 --> 00:17:40.920
And it costs them money, guys.

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:44.440
They don't do this for fun, okay?

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:54.120
Every single application that goes in to
the state planning department, it costs
anything between $500 to $1,000 per home,

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:57.800
depending on what state you're in and what
home you're applying for.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:04.040
What it also does is it gives us a gauge
on home buyer demand.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:09.240
So the demand for housing from the US
consumer.

00:18:10.040 --> 00:18:17.000
And this, as a consideration from a
traders and portfolio managers
perspective,

00:18:17.640 --> 00:18:23.080
runs in tandem with our analysis of
consumer sentiment.

00:18:23.080 --> 00:18:28.920
Because it may eventually lead to changes
in future consumer spending.

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:38.360
So if home buyers are bullish, it probably
means in the future, they're going to be
spending money.

00:18:39.080 --> 00:18:42.440
And if they're bearish, home buyers are
bearish.

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:48.680
It probably means in the future that
they're eventually going to spend less
money.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:50.200
So tightening their belts.

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:58.600
But what it also does is it gives us a
gauge on the liquidity and health of the
US credit market.

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:00.760
And it does this in two ways.

00:19:01.480 --> 00:19:03.560
First of all, the supply of loans.

00:19:04.120 --> 00:19:10.280
Developers in the US don't finance
projects with 100% equity or cash.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:18.840
They put down a certain amount of money
and they use a mix of that cash and they
borrow money.

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:19.880
They use debt.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:28.120
Additionally, the home buyers themselves,
the consumers, they don't buy houses for
cash.

00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:32.040
They buy them with debt, they buy them
with mortgages.

00:19:32.600 --> 00:19:43.720
So the building permits number gives us an
insight into these two areas in terms of
the liquidity and health of the US lending
environment,

00:19:43.720 --> 00:19:53.320
government, which is essentially the
health of the banking sector and the
credit markets and the overall economy in
the United States.

