region.

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:42.920
So if you're really interested in looking
at what's happening at the state level,

00:27:43.800 --> 00:27:49.160
there's obviously important information
that can be gleaned from that from time to
time.

00:27:49.880 --> 00:27:56.520
The biggest places that you need to be
looking at are obviously in the US, New
York, Florida,

00:27:57.960 --> 00:27:59.800
Texas and California.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:07.720
When the monthly data comes out, it's not
only useful from a trading and portfolio
management perspective

00:28:08.280 --> 00:28:14.680
to look at the headline number for the
whole of the United States, but to
potentially understand

00:28:15.240 --> 00:28:20.040
where any growth and contraction is coming
from at a state level.

00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:28.680
But for the purposes of this video and the
PTM video series, we're going to be
looking only at the headline

00:28:28.680 --> 00:28:31.880
rate of the United States as our main
gauge.

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:39.320
So let's have a look at an example of
building permits in recent history.

00:28:40.040 --> 00:28:47.640
What we've got on the screen here is the
period June 2018 to December 2020,

00:28:48.200 --> 00:28:56.920
which if you think back to video seven
with the ISM and other videos, we looked
at this period,

00:28:57.800 --> 00:29:02.680
the period August 2018 to August 2019.

00:29:02.680 --> 00:29:09.640
And if you remember, that was the period
when the ISM peaked, so in August 2018,

00:29:10.600 --> 00:29:13.080
and then went into slowdown.

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:21.320
If you recall what was going on in the
money markets during that period from
August 18 to August 19,

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:30.040
the yield curve began flattening, then the
short end of the curve inverted slightly
versus the middle.

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:34.360
So we had a slight inversion of the yield
curve there.

00:29:36.120 --> 00:29:40.120
And that occurs up until spring 2019.

00:29:40.840 --> 00:29:44.680
And if you recall, the selloff in the S&P
500

00:29:45.640 --> 00:29:51.000
happened between October 2018 and January
2019.

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:56.120
And the selloff was approximately 17 or
18%.

00:29:56.840 --> 00:30:03.320
And if you recall, when we took a snapshot
of the tens and twos in August 2019,

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:10.200
there was slight inversion, but
essentially flat, but slight inversion.

00:30:11.160 --> 00:30:15.160
And the ISM was below 50 for the first
time.

00:30:16.600 --> 00:30:23.800
Now, as you probably recall, the Fed saw
the problem coming and they cut rates in
July,

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:32.280
then in September and October 2019, in
response to the money markets chaos

00:30:32.920 --> 00:30:36.280
and the ISM manufacturing slowdown.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:44.520
Now, if we look at permits during this
period, so August 18 to July 19.

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:52.760
So leading into the August 19 ISM and
yield curve inversion.

00:30:53.880 --> 00:30:59.480
If we look during that period, August 18
up until July 19,

00:30:59.480 --> 00:31:04.600
we can see that building permits
essentially didn't grow at all.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:18.600
So if you look on the screen, in August
2018, permits were reported at 1.258
million by June 2019.

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:24.760
And remember, that June number will have
been reported in the second or third week
of July.

00:31:26.120 --> 00:31:31.800
That June number is 1.273 million building
permits issued.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:37.880
So essentially no growth over that period,
so essentially flat.

00:31:39.000 --> 00:31:46.680
But what's interesting is what happens to
building permits in July 2019 and beyond.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:58.520
So remember, the Fed cut rates in July and
the July building permits number will have
been reported in the second or third week
of August.

00:31:59.560 --> 00:32:10.760
So what we can see on the screen very
obviously is that in response to the Fed
rate cuts, permits taking off almost
immediately.

00:32:10.760 --> 00:32:18.120
We get a reading in July 2019, reported in
the second, third week of August.

00:32:18.120 --> 00:32:25.320
We get a reading in July 19 of 1.366
million building permits issued.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:31.480
And then in August, we get 1.471 million
building permits issued.

00:32:32.200 --> 00:32:49.640
And we see growth continuing up until
February 2020, when we see the COVID
shutdowns start to take effect on the
housing market and building permits
issued.

00:32:50.600 --> 00:33:00.360
And in March 2020, we see building permits
go down again and then they hit a low in
April 2020.

00:33:01.240 --> 00:33:13.320
And then they start to go higher in May,
struggling around May and June at around
the same level at 1.2 million, 1.25
million.

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:16.680
And then in July, they start to take off
again.

00:33:16.680 --> 00:33:26.120
So July 2020, and then we have a continual
rise throughout the year of 2020 for the
rest of the year.

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:37.880
Now, obviously, during that time, we had a
massive intervention from the Federal
Reserve during that period, during the
COVID lockdowns.

00:33:37.880 --> 00:33:48.360
But something else was going on as well,
there was a consumer or human response,
which also helped precipitate a boom in
permits.

00:33:49.240 --> 00:33:59.560
The lockdowns, so the extended draconian
COVID lockdowns resulted in a big increase
in interstate migration.

00:33:59.560 --> 00:34:06.200
So that's New Yorkers moving to Florida,
mostly, and Californians moving to Texas.

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:10.440
There was a big increase in interstate
migration.

00:34:10.440 --> 00:34:28.680
So that combined with record low interest
rates and big increases in the money
supply, plus the Fed and government
stability packages created incremental
demand for new housing and mortgage
refinancing.

00:34:28.680 --> 00:34:39.160
And if you recall as well, the Fed's
actions definitely steepened the yield
curve, and the S&P 500 during that time
rallied very, very hard.

00:34:40.360 --> 00:34:56.040
But interestingly, if we think about
looking at periods in time and what was
actually going on at those moments in
time, if we think of that period, August
18 to August 19, that we've looked at in
previous videos,

00:34:56.600 --> 00:35:08.280
when we look at building permits, August
18 to the June report, which was reported
in July 19, if we think of building
permits, yes, they didn't go up.

00:35:08.280 --> 00:35:10.120
Yes, they were flat.

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:13.800
But guess what, there was actually no
collapse.

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:27.800
So imagine yourself sitting at that moment
in time, analysing this building permits
data and all of the other leading
indicators, and every week you're getting
new and more and more information.

00:35:27.800 --> 00:35:33.960
So in that period, August 18 to June 19,
there was no collapse in building permits.

00:35:35.320 --> 00:35:48.200
The S&P 500 sold off between October 18
and January 19 of around 17-18%, and the
ISM was in decline from peak.

00:35:49.240 --> 00:35:57.080
And the ISM manufacturing number did go
below 50 for the first time in August 19.

00:35:57.080 --> 00:36:06.680
But if you recall, when we looked at the
UMCSI, the consumer data, there wasn't a
collapse in consumer spending.

00:36:06.680 --> 00:36:13.000
And also in the services sector, there was
no collapse in the service sector either.

00:36:13.000 --> 00:36:17.400
So remember, when we looked at the ISM
non-manufacturing report.

00:36:17.960 --> 00:36:29.560
So there were signs there, guys, that even
though the ISM, the manufacturing number,
was in decline from peak and went below 50
for the first time in August 19,

00:36:29.560 --> 00:36:36.680
the US consumer building permits and the
services sector hadn't collapsed.

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:39.720
They were holding up and they were still
very strong.

00:36:41.400 --> 00:36:50.520
The Fed took action because of stresses in
the bond market and because of a
manufacturing slowdown in the US.

00:36:51.560 --> 00:37:01.640
Building permits actually held steady
throughout, similar to the US consumer,
the UMCSI and the US services sector.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:27.080
So this is a good example, guys, of
looking at a moment in history and looking
at a leading indicator and putting it into
the context during that moment in history
with what was going on in the economy
overall and in the context of what was
happening with other leading indicators.

00:37:27.080 --> 00:37:48.760
By going back in time and looking at
trends and turning points in economic
history in the business cycle and putting
it into context with what was happening in
the economy and with other leading
indicators at the time,

00:37:48.760 --> 00:38:04.040
what you're actually doing is you're
becoming a student of business cycle
history and economic history and what
you're actually doing is you're honing
your skills for understanding leading
indicators and understanding the economy.

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:30.040
So in the future, when you actually sit
down at any one point in time and you look
at a particular leading indicator and a
collection of them and you look at the
context of what is occurring in the world
and in the economy at the time because
you've practiced and looked at past
business cycles and understand business
cycle history,

00:38:30.040 --> 00:38:42.040
you're going to understand what is
happening in the current context at any
one moment in time a hell of a lot better
than somebody who has no idea what's going
on at all.

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:47.040
This is obviously where you can get an
edge in the market.

00:38:47.040 --> 00:39:00.040
Now, when it comes to building permits and
the history of the data, we put together
the all time permits chart here and this
is available in the downloads area.

00:39:00.040 --> 00:39:12.040
And you can denote some very obvious
visual traits of the building permits data
when looking at the chart.

00:39:12.040 --> 00:39:15.040
Very obviously, there's peaks and troughs.

00:39:15.040 --> 00:39:20.040
We've also put a long term average trend
line on the chart.

00:39:20.040 --> 00:39:29.040
So visually, you can see the long term
average trend line is between 1.3 million
and 1.4 million.

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:41.040
You can also see that there's, if we look
at troughs at the bottom of the chart, you
can also see that visually there's some
very obvious levels for troughs in
building permits data.

00:39:41.040 --> 00:40:00.040
You can see 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 data points
before the global financial crisis where
building permits were essentially between
7,000 and 800,000 where they troughed in
previous business cycles.

00:40:00.040 --> 00:40:14.040
And then in the global financial crisis,
building permits bottomed in the low
500,000 and we're bottoming basically
between 500,000 and 600,000 permits
issued.

00:40:14.040 --> 00:40:26.040
When we look at the peaks, we can
basically see peaks at 2 million permits
and above throughout the permits data
history.

00:40:26.040 --> 00:40:42.040
So for example, if you look at the global
financial crisis going into it, you can
see that permits were above the 2 million
mark for quite a long time.

00:40:42.040 --> 00:40:55.040
Essentially, permits were above 2 million
for most of 2004 and all of 2005 and the
beginning part of 2006.

00:40:55.040 --> 00:41:07.040
It was in May 2006 when permits actually
fell below 2 million and then they went
below 1.9 million in June 2006.

00:41:07.040 --> 00:41:20.040
So that's pretty amazing because when we
think about earlier, when we looked at our
lead times for permits to starts and then
completions,

00:41:20.040 --> 00:41:27.040
we denoted that building permits were
leading 12 to 16 months on completions.

00:41:27.040 --> 00:41:42.040
And this time period is born out here if
you look at the channel for the upswing in
housing permits from the early 90s all the
way up to 2006.

00:41:42.040 --> 00:41:58.040
The channel was broken and we were being
warned about a big slowdown in the housing
market, a very meaningful slowdown in the
housing market in the United States in May
and June 2006.

00:41:58.040 --> 00:42:06.040
And that time horizon is born out because
the financial crisis happened or started
happening 12 months later.

00:42:06.040 --> 00:42:15.040
And then 16, 18 months later, that's when
the financial crisis really started to get
going in the second half of 2007.

00:42:15.040 --> 00:42:28.040
In more recent history, you can see on the
right hand side of the chart, permits
coming out of the financial crisis where
they troughed in the low 500s.

00:42:28.040 --> 00:42:35.040
And then permits going up consistently in
that channel.

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:55.040
And apart from a blip up, an aggressive
blip up in 2015, permits didn't really
recover back to the long term average
trend line of between 13, 1.3 million and
1.4 million.

00:42:55.040 --> 00:43:00.040
Until 2016, 2017.

00:43:00.040 --> 00:43:15.040
So it took a long time for the housing
market to recover to a long term average
trend in terms of building permits coming
out of the financial crisis.

00:43:15.040 --> 00:43:44.040
And looking at very recent history, we can
obviously see that periods that we were
looking at in 2018, 2019 and 2020, where
we have the building permits number above
long term trend, quite significantly above
and then coming down in 2020 for the COVID
collapse and then rallying after the COVID
reopening.

00:43:44.040 --> 00:44:05.040
So you can go back in the downloads area
into the data, not just looking at the
chart, but also looking at the actual
columns and rows with all the data in it
and just have a scroll through, have a
look at previous lows and peaks.

00:44:05.040 --> 00:44:19.040
So previous drops and peaks in building
permits, put them into context, put them
into context of what was going on at the
time in the economy and also what was
happening with other leading indicators.

00:44:19.040 --> 00:44:22.040
Become a student of the business cycle.

00:44:22.040 --> 00:44:29.040
Something else that's in the downloads
area is the building permits month on
month percentage change.

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:32.040
And that's with all data history.

00:44:32.040 --> 00:44:39.040
If you look at the bottom of the chart,
you can see the percentage change on the
right hand side.

00:44:39.040 --> 00:44:50.040
So on the right hand axis and the changes
are denoted visually to help visually with
positives in green and negatives in red.

00:44:50.040 --> 00:45:01.040
And you can see peaks and troughs in that
type of performance as well on the month
on month changes, percentage changes in
the data.

00:45:01.040 --> 00:45:21.040
So you can see, for example, when we have
a monthly change that's at 20% or above,
that tends to be a monthly move where we
don't see moves after that following as
big as that move.

00:45:21.040 --> 00:45:24.040
So a plus 20% in the positives.

00:45:24.040 --> 00:45:29.040
And in the negatives, it's the same, but a
negative 20%.

00:45:29.040 --> 00:45:38.040
Whenever we get a month that's negative
20% change in building permits, months
that follow are not that extreme.

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:50.040
We've also, in the downloads area, got
data and got the charts for building
permits compared to GDP growth.

00:45:50.040 --> 00:46:03.040
So here we're looking at a year on year
percentage change in building permits
versus year on year percentage change of
GDP growth.

00:46:03.040 --> 00:46:14.040
And you can clearly see that when building
permits is in the positives, we're getting
GDP growth growing in the same direction
in the positives.

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:27.040
And when building permit changes, year on
year go negative soon after we see GDP
growth year on year percentage going in
the same direction and going negative.

00:46:27.040 --> 00:46:29.040
But not all the time.

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:33.040
So we don't look at this chart and say
there's a rule book.

00:46:33.040 --> 00:46:42.040
What we have to do is always use the data
in conjunction with other leading
indicators as well.

00:46:42.040 --> 00:46:54.040
Of course, if we see building permits year
on year percentage changes going negative,
we take note of that and we look at it in
the context of other leading indicators
and what's happening in the economy.

00:46:54.040 --> 00:47:04.040
We don't have a rule book which says GDP
growth will absolutely be negative if
building permits year on year percentage
change is negative.

00:47:04.040 --> 00:47:14.040
And we also don't have a rule book for
exactly what's going to happen in the
direction it's going to be and the actual
magnitude of a move as well.

00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:18.040
And also, there isn't a rule book for the
exact time lag.

00:47:18.040 --> 00:47:23.040
Everything has to be taken into context.

00:47:23.040 --> 00:47:43.040
What we've also done when we look at
building permits leading the S&P 500,
we've also done a useful exercise here of
looking at the 12 month moving average of
building permits year on year percentage
change.

00:47:43.040 --> 00:48:04.040
So we've taken that same data, the year on
year percentage change, and we're looking
at a 12 month moving average of that data
and comparing it to a 12 month moving
average of the S&P 500 year on year
percentage data, putting that on the same
chart.

00:48:04.040 --> 00:48:31.040
And visually, you can see from this
exercise that they both move in the same
direction, sometimes at the same time and
sometimes with a time lag where permits is
leading the S&P 500 direction and
potential S&P 500 annual year on year
percentage change.

00:48:31.040 --> 00:49:00.040
But again, there's no definitive rule book
here that says if permits are doing
something like breaking to the upside to
go into the positive in terms of year on
year percentage change or breaking to the
downside of year on year percentage
change, but we have to do a certain action
in the S&P 500.

00:49:00.040 --> 00:49:09.040
So permits, again, permits, again, have to
be taken into context with everything else
or other leading indicators.

00:49:09.040 --> 00:49:25.040
And that overall should give us a very,
very good idea of the directional bias
that we would have in our portfolios and
potentially what to be long and what to be
short in our portfolios.

00:49:25.040 --> 00:49:34.040
Now, you'll also find in the downloads
area in the spreadsheet that we've got the
data there for housing starts.

00:49:34.040 --> 00:49:40.040
And we've got the data also for housing
completions in your own time.

00:49:40.040 --> 00:49:44.040
You can go through that data, but we've
got the visuals there as well for you.

00:49:44.040 --> 00:49:52.040
So you can learn about the data and the
data history and also how to put these
charts together.

00:49:52.040 --> 00:49:57.040
And you can also, obviously, on a monthly
basis, use these spreadsheets.

00:49:57.040 --> 00:50:01.040
And when you update your data, update your
charts.

00:50:01.040 --> 00:50:05.040
So that will be something you'll be doing
on a monthly basis.

00:50:05.040 --> 00:50:10.040
And yes, that's the self-starter
mentality, guys.

00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:19.040
This is showing up regularly to get
consistent returns in your portfolio.

00:50:19.040 --> 00:50:20.040
Guess what?

00:50:20.040 --> 00:50:25.040
You're going to have to show up
consistently and do the work.

00:50:25.040 --> 00:50:33.040
Now, I mentioned earlier about the global
financial crisis, 2008, 2009.

00:50:33.040 --> 00:50:39.040
Let's have a little look at a bit of
business cycle history.

00:50:39.040 --> 00:50:46.040
And again, go through this exercise of
looking at turning points and trends in a
leading indicator.

00:50:47.040 --> 00:50:52.040
Let's have a look at building permits and
what was going on back then.

00:50:53.040 --> 00:51:06.040
Obviously, if we're going to be analyzing
building permits and attempting to gauge
its usefulness as a leading indicator,

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:15.040
the most obvious place to look in business
cycle history would actually be the
housing crash of 2008, 2009.

00:51:15.040 --> 00:51:20.040
And you can see on the chart when we look
at the data for building permits.

00:51:20.040 --> 00:51:36.040
So in the second column, you can see that
building permits, the low was 521,000
seasonally adjusted annualized rate in
April, 2009.

00:51:36.040 --> 00:51:39.040
And remember, that will have been reported
in May.

00:51:39.040 --> 00:51:52.040
And if we fast forward a bit in time, we
can see the building permits number
getting stronger, but it's struggling
essentially.

00:51:52.040 --> 00:52:04.040
And then there was a near term high put in
for the month of March, 2010, which was
687,000 seasonally adjusted annualized
rate.

00:52:04.040 --> 00:52:09.040
And the number then goes lower.

00:52:09.040 --> 00:52:13.040
And it really struggles for a long time.

00:52:13.040 --> 00:52:20.040
And that's basically because there were so
many homes that were on the market.

00:52:20.040 --> 00:52:26.040
And there was a lot of inventory to get
through, especially foreclosures.

00:52:26.040 --> 00:52:31.040
And it took a long time for new homes.

00:52:31.040 --> 00:52:39.040
So building permits to come back to the
level it was even in 2010.

00:52:39.040 --> 00:53:00.040
So the permits number, the breakout was
actually around February 2012, where we
had three months in a row of breaking the
previous April 2010 resistance, which gave
us confirmation that we had a breakout.

00:53:00.040 --> 00:53:16.040
And that was almost a full two years after
the high was put in post the trough in
April 2010.

00:53:16.040 --> 00:53:21.040
So the high was put in in April 2010, post
the trough.

00:53:21.040 --> 00:53:27.040
So we had the trough March 09, 521,000.

00:53:27.040 --> 00:53:39.040
We had the March 2010 report, which is
reported in April 2010, which came out at
687,000.

00:53:39.040 --> 00:53:46.040
And then it took a full almost two years
to get back above that level.

00:53:46.040 --> 00:53:51.040
Now, putting this into a bit of context of
what was going on elsewhere.

00:53:51.040 --> 00:54:10.040
If you recall back to the US consumer
video, the UMCSI video, where we
highlighted that the US consumer, after
the global financial crisis, didn't really
recover until the first half of 2012.

00:54:10.040 --> 00:54:20.040
So if you recall, QE2 was terminated June
29, 2012 by the Fed on the right hand
side.

00:54:20.040 --> 00:54:25.040
This was the data screenshot from the
